# Graph traversal: BFS and DFS

Representations: adjacency list (O(V + E) space, good for sparse graphs) vs adjacency matrix (O(V²), O(1) edge test).

## BFS

Queue. Visits nodes in order of distance (edge count) from the source, so it gives shortest paths in unweighted graphs. O(V + E).

## DFS

Stack or recursion. Classifies edges into tree, back, forward and cross edges. A back edge means a cycle (directed graph). Uses:
- topological sort: reverse post-order of DFS on a DAG;
- strongly connected components (Tarjan or Kosaraju);
- articulation points and bridges.

## Pitfalls

- Recursion depth on a long chain blows the stack; use an explicit stack for big inputs.
- Mark nodes visited when you push them in BFS, not when you pop, or you'll enqueue duplicates.

The kernel's path resolution is a walk down one path of the directory tree; `find` is a DFS of it.
